WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.400
Who signs in to the console, and how.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.729
Version 26.1 supports single sign-on to
the web console through OIDC or SAML, and

3
00:00:08.729 --> 00:00:11.989
CyberArk's vendor table lists Microsoft

4
00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:15.537
Entra ID as supported
for SAML, OIDC and SCIM.

5
00:00:15.537 --> 00:00:19.812
The MMC snap-ins sign in
with the OIDC device flow.

6
00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:23.369
CyberArk also documents
client-certificate login:

7
00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.753
you enable certificate authentication,
choose the trusted CAs,

8
00:00:27.753 --> 00:00:32.882
and set the IIS site to require client
certificates; the platform takes the

9
00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:37.762
identity from the certificate's UPN,
email, common name, or SID extension.

10
00:00:37.762 --> 00:00:41.863
What CyberArk doesn't document
is PIV or CAC by name.

11
00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:46.373
So the program's design guidance is to
federate the web console to Entra ID,

12
00:00:46.373 --> 00:00:51.328
and let administrators present their
PIV card through Entra certificate-based

13
00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:54.212
authentication, as the
Federal Modernization

14
00:00:54.212 --> 00:00:56.874
Workbook's Book 07
Chapter 06 configures it.

15
00:00:56.874 --> 00:01:02.267
That keeps one CBA trust store and
Conditional Access in front of the console,

16
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.637
rather than a second,
separate trust store in IIS.

17
00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:08.963
The native client-certificate
login stays a fallback.

18
00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:11.746
Master Admin is eligible, not standing,

19
00:01:11.746 --> 00:01:14.963
and the local master
admin is the break-glass.

20
00:01:14.963 --> 00:01:16.138
Then the API.

21
00:01:16.138 --> 00:01:18.905
Automation authenticates with OAuth tokens

22
00:01:18.905 --> 00:01:21.526
from the platform's authentication server.

23
00:01:21.526 --> 00:01:25.422
The defaults are a ninety-day
access token and a one-year grant,

24
00:01:25.422 --> 00:01:28.250
and the program's guidance
is to shorten both.

25
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.475
API keys are no longer supported.

26
00:01:30.475 --> 00:01:36.406
Sources: CyberArk 26.1 What's New; Login
experience; Vendor support for SSO;

27
00:01:36.406 --> 00:01:40.162
Configuration Console Authentication node;

28
00:01:40.162 --> 00:01:46.982
Configuring certificate authentication in
the platform and IIS; Setting up OAuth;

29
00:01:46.982 --> 00:01:52.970
Authentication options; Deprecated
features; Book 07, Chapters 05 and 06.
